# Design: Face-Verified Physical Access Control for Hermes — Current State → Target State

> **Status (2026-08-06): design snapshot from the evening before the build — kept for the
> decision trail; the README is the current truth.** Phase A shipped the next day as
> `face-cpu` (InsightFace buffalo_s, CPU ONNX — built, tested, verified live; see
> [README § Face recognition](../README.md#face-recognition-face-cpu)). Two claims below are
> superseded: the threshold is now **0.43, measured** (not the uncalibrated 0.5), and the
> suite is **327 tests**. Of the two review gaps this doc folds into Phase A:
> backend-failure signaling shipped (`degradedFrom` reaches the persisted state and the
> wall/phone captions, and a blind sentry never suppresses a page — `access/sentry.ts`);
> the enrollment endpoint still accepts a client-supplied embedding and is mitigated
> operationally instead — the phone enrol form stays disabled, enrolment is offline via
> `scripts/enroll.py`, and the shared secret is required off-loopback
> ([RUNBOOK §9](RUNBOOK.md#9-venue-preflight--the-pre-demo-checklist)).

## Context

Hermes's access-control subsystem (`mcp-tools/src/access/`) exists to close a gap
`docs/POSITIONING.md` §7 had promised but never built: "observe → explain → recommend →
**human approves** → act." The presence trigger, phone capture UI, roster storage, decision
matrix, and Telegram alerting are all built and tested (PROGRESS.md #12/#13, 242/242 tests
passing at last count). The one missing piece is the actual face-recognition model — today
every capture resolves as `unknown` via a detection-only stub, because `ACCESS_IDENTITY_METHOD`
has no working `face-npu`/`face-cpu` backend (`ACCESS_VISION_SCRIPT` doesn't exist).

This session's conversation worked through what should fill that gap, landing on: pre-enrolled
face embeddings (never images), matched via cosine similarity against a captured photo, feeding
the existing human-approval flow. Two follow-up reviews (external, "cline") of the first version
of this plan surfaced two real trust-boundary gaps in the *existing* code that the original plan
under-scoped — a Python backend failure degrading silently into an ordinary "unknown person"
result, and the enrollment endpoint accepting a client-supplied embedding with no server-side
verification. Both are folded into Phase A below rather than kept as a separate checklist,
because a broken backend that looks like a working one, and a roster that trusts whatever vector
a client sends, are the two ways this feature could produce a misleading demo.

