## Research findings that constrain the design

1. **NPU contention is a documented, reproducible failure mode on this hardware**, not a
   theoretical risk. `docs/BENCHMARKS.md` / `llm-serving-bench/RESULTS.md` record GenieX
   crashing twice when a second Hexagon-touching process ran concurrently ("second Hexagon
   process destabilizes the DSP"). Any design that puts a face model on the *laptop's* NPU
   at the same time GenieX may be serving a turn inherits this risk directly.
2. **The phone's NPU is architecturally idle today** and is the same silicon family
   (Qualcomm Hexagon inside Snapdragon 8 Elite) as the laptop's Hexagon v73 — so moving face
   recognition there uses the same AI Hub / QNN toolchain conceptually, not a different vendor
   stack. This is a genuine "all-Snapdragon, every NPU doing real inference" story, which
   `docs/HARDWARE_UTILIZATION.md`'s own scoring rubric explicitly rewards (flagged there as a
   current gap — phone NPU idle).
3. **Open/unresolved, needs a 5-minute check before it goes on any slide**: the Hexagon
   version for the 8 Elite is inconsistently documented across two Qualcomm-adjacent sources
   already cited in this repo (`docs/PHONE_PLAN_2026-08-05.md` — v73 in one tutorial, v79 per
   llama.cpp's own docs). Resolve before quoting a specific NPU generation for the phone.
4. **Phone-NPU inference requires a native app or WebNN pipeline.** This contradicts the
   phone's current stated design ("There is no app to build," `phone/README.md`) and is a
   scope increase comparable to the existing MCP tools build, not a small addition — nothing
   for it exists today (no app skeleton, no model conversion, no packaging).
5. **Face embedding models are much smaller than the LLM concern that shelved on-phone
   inference before.** The earlier "phone on-device LLM" idea was shelved over a 12GB RAM
   floor for 3B+ models exactly matching the S25 Ultra's 12GB (`docs/HARDWARE_UTILIZATION.md`).
   Face embedding models are tens of MB, not billions of parameters — that specific blocker
   likely doesn't apply, but the actual model's footprint should be checked, not assumed.
6. **No face-quality gating exists anywhere in the code today.** `detectOnly()` (the stub)
   always returns exactly one face with no size/blur/angle/multi-face checks. Any real vision
   backend needs to add these itself — "call model, get vector" is not sufficient for a usable
   feature, model choice aside.
7. **Liveness/anti-spoof is out of scope.** Nothing today or in this plan defends against a
   printed photo or a phone screen held up to the camera. Stated explicitly as an accepted
   limitation for a hackathon demo, not silently absent.
8. **A crashed/unavailable backend must never look like a normal "unknown person" result.**
   `identify.ts` already carries a `degradedFrom` string through to the phone UI as a caption,
   but the decision matrix (`decide.ts`) has no input for it today, so the verdict and severity
   are computed identically whether the model ran and said "stranger" or never ran at all. A
   demo that silently treats "the model is broken" as "access denied" is misleading, not safe.
9. **Client-supplied enrollment vectors are a live trust-boundary gap, not a future concern.**
   `accessEnrol()` persists whatever numeric array a POST body contains as a face template.
   Combined with `ACCESS_SHARED_SECRET` being opt-in, anyone who can reach the endpoint (and,
   even on loopback-only, anyone driving the UI) can enroll an arbitrary vector as a "known"
   face without ever having their face embedded by the real model.
10. **A threshold picked from 2-3 enrolled people is a smoke test, not a calibration.** Real
    threshold selection needs multiple captures per enrolled person (varying lighting/angle/
    distance) plus deliberate impostor attempts, so the false-accept behavior is at least
    observed once, even without a formal statistical evaluation harness.

## Target architecture (end state)

```
Phase A/B (laptop):                       Phase C (stretch — phone NPU):
Phone (browser, phone.html) →              Phone (native app/WebNN, NEW)
  JPEG → downscale → base64                  on-device model → embedding vector
  POST /api/access/capture {imageBase64}      POST /api/access/capture {embedding, model, modelVersion}
       ↓                                          ↓ (authenticated, session-correlated;
Laptop: identify.ts::fromPython                    verification only — enrollment stays
  spawns ACCESS_VISION_SCRIPT (NEW)                 on /api/access/enroll, see Phase C)
  runs on CPU (A) or NPU (B)                     ↓
       ↓                                   Laptop: identify.ts (unchanged downstream)
  identityStatus: resolved |                 matchEmbedding() — unchanged, already built
    no_match | capture_rejected |                 ↓
    backend_unavailable | backend_error     decideAccess() → notifyChallenge() → phone approve/deny
       ↓                                     (all unchanged, already built)
decideAccess() → ...
```

`phone.html`'s capture UI is **reused, not left unchanged** — Phase A adds new result states
(`capture_rejected`, `backend_unavailable`/`backend_error`) that the existing page doesn't render
today and must be extended to show. Everything *downstream* of "produce a validated embedding
tagged with an explicit `identityStatus`" is identical regardless of phase — that stability is
why the phases can be built and demoed independently, each a strict improvement on the last, none
a rewrite of the others.

## Phased build plan — build in this order, next session(s)

### Phase A — `face-cpu` on the laptop, with the safety slice included (build first)

This phase is the full vertical slice: model script, structured outcomes threaded through the
decision matrix, safe enrollment, and a provisional threshold. Not split into a separate
"Phase 0" — kept as one phase so the first real model integration is safe by construction rather
than safe in a follow-up.

**A1 — Model process contract and script**
- Write `ACCESS_VISION_SCRIPT`: a Python script on the existing stdin/stdout process contract
  in `identify.ts::fromPython`, tightened to:
  - stdin: `{"imageBase64": "..."}`
  - stdout on success: `{"ok": true, "embeddings": [[...]], "boxes": [[x,y,w,h]], "device": "cpu", "model": "<id>", "modelVersion": "<ver>", "detectedFaceCount": 1}`
  - stdout on rejection: `{"ok": false, "error": "no_face" | "multiple_faces" | "face_too_small" | "poor_quality" | "invalid_image", "detectedFaceCount": <n>}`
  - **`detectedFaceCount` is required on every response, success or rejection** — see A2 for why
    this must survive independently of whether identity matching itself succeeded.
  - stdout is JSON only; all diagnostics go to stderr
- **`runWithStdin` currently enforces a timeout only — it does not bound stdin or stdout size.**
  (Corrected from the prior draft, which incorrectly claimed an existing size limit.) Add before
  wiring this in: a base64-length/decoded-byte check on the image before spawning the process, a
  decoded-image dimension/pixel ceiling enforced inside the Python script, and a stdout/stderr
  byte ceiling on the Node side that kills the child if exceeded — the HTTP layer's 8MB body cap
  does not protect this path, since a direct/internal caller can bypass it.
- Validate `ACCESS_VISION_TIMEOUT_MS` itself is finite and positive before using it.
- **One shared model-configuration source of truth**, not two independently-maintained values in
  TypeScript and Python: define `ACCESS_MODEL_ID`, `ACCESS_MODEL_VERSION`, `ACCESS_EMBEDDING_DIM`
  as env vars that both the Python script and the TypeScript validation layer (A2) read. Env vars
  are an intentional hackathon-scoped choice — a checked-in model manifest file (id/version/dim/
  normalization/detector, validated against at load time) is the more robust version of this and
  worth doing if time allows, but isn't required for Phase A to be safe.
- The script echoes `model`/`modelVersion`/`device` on every response so the server can catch a
  drift between what it expects and what actually ran.
- **Validate the script's output shape strictly on the TypeScript side, as a runtime type guard,
  not a cast** — `ok` is a boolean; `device` is `"cpu"` or `"npu"` and nothing else (today
  `fromPython()` treats *any* value other than the literal string `"cpu"` — including `undefined`
  or a typo — as `"face-npu"`; that must become a rejection, not a silent NPU claim); `model`/
  `modelVersion` are present and match `ACCESS_MODEL_ID`/`ACCESS_MODEL_VERSION` on success; every
  embedding has exactly `ACCESS_EMBEDDING_DIM` finite numbers; `boxes.length` (if present) matches
  `embeddings.length`; `detectedFaceCount` is present and is a non-negative integer. Any violation
  — wrong shape, non-finite numbers, a dimension mismatch, an unrecognized `device` — becomes
  `backend_error` (A2), never an empty/unknown face and never a claimed NPU run.
- Use a stock ONNX face-detection + embedding model, ONNX Runtime, CPU execution provider —
  no QNN, no NPU, zero contention with GenieX by construction. Check the model's license before
  committing its weights to the repo.
- Implement quality gating inside the script before embedding: reject on no-face-detected, face
  too small, and (if the detector gives landmarks) too oblique an angle. Basic blur check
  (Laplacian variance) if time allows. **Do not reject `multiple_faces` without also reporting
  `detectedFaceCount`** — see A2, this count feeds tailgating detection regardless of whether
  identity resolution proceeds.
- Add a standalone health-check invocation (run the script against one test image with no
  Hermes/dashboard involved) so a broken environment is caught before it's wired in.

**A2 — Detected-face count is not the same signal as matched identity (critical correction —
the original draft's `multiple_faces` → `capture_rejected` handling would have silently disabled
tailgating detection, an existing, demoed security feature)**
- `decideAccess()`'s tailgating check today is literally `faceCount = input.faces.length`
  (`decide.ts`). If a multi-face capture is treated purely as a rejected capture with zero
  entries in `faces[]`, `faceCount` becomes `0` and tailgating can never fire again for that
  capture — a real regression against `docs/PROGRESS.md`'s own "centerpiece" feature, not a
  gap in new functionality.
- Fix: **separate "how many faces were detected" from "which faces were successfully matched
  to an identity."** Thread `detectedFaceCount` (from A1) into the decision input alongside
  `faces[]`, and have `decideAccess()`'s tailgating branch key off `detectedFaceCount`, not
  `faces.length`. A rejected capture (0 embeddings because the backend declined to embed) can
  still carry `detectedFaceCount: 3` and correctly trigger tailgating against door-entry count,
  even though no individual identity was resolved that tick.
- Policy: one detected face → attempt embedding/matching as normal. Multiple detected faces →
  preserve the count and boxes for the tailgating check regardless of whether embedding was
  attempted; still request a retake/re-capture for the identity-resolution side of the decision,
  since tailgating and "who are these people" are separate questions that can both be true at
  once.

**A3 — No silent backend fallback, via an explicit decision input (the fix cline's review first
flagged, tightened across two follow-up passes)**
- Add a typed field the decision matrix can actually branch on — `approvalRequired: true` alone
  does **not** distinguish a backend failure from an ordinary `challenge`, since a plain unknown
  face already sets that flag. Add:
  ```ts
  type IdentityStatus =
    | "resolved"            // model ran, produced a usable embedding (known or unknown)
    | "no_match"             // model ran, embedding didn't clear the roster threshold
    | "capture_rejected"     // model ran, refused the input itself (no_face/face_too_small/etc.)
    | "backend_unavailable"  // script missing/not configured
    | "backend_error";       // script crashed, timed out, or returned malformed/invalid output
  ```
  A discriminated union (status tag plus only the fields valid for that status, so e.g. `matched`
  can't exist with an empty `faces[]`) is the more defensively-typed shape and is recommended if
  there's time to build it that way; a single enum plus optional fields is an acceptable
  simplification for the hackathon timeline and is what the rest of this plan assumes by default.
- Wire this into `decideAccess()`'s input (`DecideAccessInput`) alongside `faces`/
  `detectedFaceCount`/`captured`, and give each status its own outcome:
  - `no_match` → ordinary `challenge`, as today.
  - `capture_rejected` → `pending-capture` with a retake-oriented reason for `no_face`/
    `face_too_small`/`poor_quality`/`invalid_image` specifically. Do not set warning severity or
    require approval on the very first rejected attempt the same way an unanswered challenge
    does after the grace period — a bad photo is not evidence of anything yet; let the existing
    grace-period escalation in `decide.ts` continue to apply if retakes keep failing.
  - `multiple_faces` is **not** folded into plain `capture_rejected` — see A2. It carries its own
    handling: preserve `detectedFaceCount`, evaluate tailgating, and separately still ask for a
    one-person-at-a-time retake for identity resolution.
  - `backend_unavailable` / `backend_error` → a verdict/severity visibly distinct from all of the
    above, and specifically must **never suppress escalation** (a broken verifier is not "the
    on-call responding") and should not enter an ordinary retake loop, since retrying against a
    dead backend is not meaningful. **Decision needed at implementation time, not deferred**:
    either add a new `AccessVerdict` value (e.g. `"verification-unavailable"`) for maximum
    clarity, or reuse `challenge`/`pending-capture` and let `identityStatus` alone carry the
    distinction. The new-enum-value option has real, wider blast radius — every `AccessVerdict`
    consumer needs updating: `phone.html`'s `VERDICT_TEXT` map, `dashboard/snapshot.ts`'s labels,
    `decide.test.ts`'s table tests, suppression logic/tests, notification wording, `state.ts`
    fixtures. Given hackathon time constraints, **default to reusing an existing verdict** and
    let `identityStatus` do the distinguishing — but if that route is taken, the phone UI and
    notification text must explicitly branch on `identityStatus`, not just the verdict, or the
    headline text will still read as an ordinary `challenge` and the whole point of this fix is
    lost. Only add a new enum value if time allows for updating all of the above consumers.
- Treat `degradedFrom` as a **human-readable diagnostic string only, never the machine-readable
  contract** — sanitize and length-limit it, and drive all actual branching off `identityStatus`.
- **Persist the typed status in the audit trail, not just a live caption.** Today `degradedFrom`
  flows `IdentifyResult → AccessSentry.captured.degradedFrom → AccessView.degradedFrom → phone
  caption` and never reaches `AccessEvent`, `applyDecision()`, or `access.json` — a demo replay of
  the audit log after the fact can't tell "the model said stranger" apart from "the model didn't
  run." Add `identityStatus` (and an optional short error code) as fields on `AccessEvent` itself,
  set them in `applyDecision()`, and let `reasons[]` continue to carry the human-readable summary.

**A4 — Roster model-compatibility enforcement (critical correction — the existing `method` field
documents a safety property `matchEmbedding()` doesn't actually implement)**
- `RosterEntry.method`'s doc comment already says it exists "so a mixed roster cannot silently
  form" — but `matchEmbedding()` compares an incoming vector against *every* roster entry
  regardless of `method`/model identity; a dimension mismatch happens to return similarity `0`,
  but two different models that happen to share an embedding dimension would be compared as if
  compatible, and nothing stops it.
- Fix in the same pass as A1's model-config work: extend `RosterEntry` to carry `model`/
  `modelVersion` (not just `method`), and have `matchEmbedding()` (or its caller) skip/reject
  roster entries whose recorded model doesn't match the currently-configured `ACCESS_MODEL_ID`/
  `ACCESS_MODEL_VERSION`, rather than comparing across incompatible models silently. A QR-badge
  roster entry (`embedding: [1]`) must never be compared against a real face embedding — the
  method/model check should reject that case outright rather than mathematically produce a
  similarity score for it.
- Prefer failing loudly (a startup or match-time warning that the roster contains entries from
  an unrecognized model) over silently dropping entries — a demo where half the roster quietly
  stopped matching is worse than one that visibly complains once.

**A5 — Enrollment as a trust boundary, not a UI tweak; and a reusable inference boundary (the
enrollment fix from cline's review, tightened with the reusability gap the third pass surfaced)**
- Stop treating `/api/access/enroll` as accepting a caller-supplied embedding as the normal
  path. The normal enrollment flow should submit an **image**, run it through the same face
  backend used for verification, and persist only the resulting embedding.
- **This needs a real refactor, not a call into existing code as-is**: `AccessSentry.capture()`
  requires an open challenge (`if (!this.state.pending) return {ok: false, ...}`), and
  `fromPython()` is private inside `identify.ts` — so there is currently no path to run the
  vision backend against an enrollment photo without an access challenge being open. Extract a
  reusable inference entry point (e.g. `inferFace(image): VisionResult`) that both
  `identifyFaces()` (challenge-gated, as today) and a new enrollment path can call, so enrollment
  doesn't depend on `AccessSentry`'s challenge state at all.
- **Split enrollment by mode rather than inferring intent from a shared shape**: use distinct
  endpoints/payloads for face enrollment (`{name, imageBase64}`, routed through the new inference
  entry point) versus the existing QR-badge claim path (`{name}` only, `method: "qr-badge"`,
  unit-vector convention). Do not let one endpoint accept both a real embedding and a placeholder
  vector under the same shape — that ambiguity is exactly how a future caller could pass a
  hand-crafted vector through what looks like the badge path.
- **Concrete, explicit auth policy** (the current `authorized()` gate is one uniform check across
  all write routes and is fully open whenever `ACCESS_SHARED_SECRET` is unset — there is no
  existing per-route enforcement to "preserve," this needs an actual code change, e.g. a
  `secretRequired: true` option on the `guarded()` wrapper): `/api/access/enroll` (both modes)
  requires `ACCESS_SHARED_SECRET` to be configured and supplied, unconditionally, regardless of
  bind host — fail closed with a clear setup error if no secret is configured at all. Capture and
  approve can remain governed by the existing global-secret behavior. Update the startup warning
  accordingly. Note for the demo: if the secret is embedded in a URL the phone itself holds, this
  protects against casual network callers, not someone who can inspect that page — call it a
  shared demo secret, not administrator authentication, in whatever docs describe it.
- Validate on the server: embedding dimension matches `ACCESS_EMBEDDING_DIM`, all values finite,
  non-zero norm, and `model`/`modelVersion` match the configured values (ties to A4). Reject
  anything else rather than silently accepting it.
- If a direct-vector enrollment path is kept for development/testing, isolate and label it
  explicitly as privileged/dev-only, compiled or enabled only behind an explicit flag — never the
  default path a real enrollment goes through.
- **Update `phone.html`'s copy once the UI changes** — it currently states "every access decision
  is made by a human reviewing the captured photo," which will no longer be true once known faces
  can produce an automatic `clear` and given images are discarded rather than shown back to the
  approver. Describe the operator as reviewing the identity result, reasons, and incident context.
- **Demo allowlist — names to be confirmed with the project owner before this section is
  finalized.** The Phase A demo roster is intended to be a small, fixed set of named authorized
  people (placeholder count: three), not an open-ended "enroll whoever's around" roster. Once
  confirmed:
  - List the exact names here, obtain each person's consent before enrollment, and capture
    multiple enrollment images per person under varied lighting/angle/distance/eyewear.
  - Run every enrollment image through the same detector/quality-gating/embedding path used for
    verification (A5's `inferFace()`) — never a different, looser path for enrollment than for
    matching.
  - **Multiple enrollment images per person require an explicit aggregation policy — the current
    roster does not support this and would silently keep only the last photo without one.**
    `RosterEntry` today is one embedding per name, and `upsertRoster()` replaces-by-name on
    re-enrollment (`roster.ts`) — capturing "several photos" with no further change means all but
    the final capture are discarded, silently. Fix: for each accepted enrollment photo, produce a
    normalized embedding, average the accepted embeddings for that person, L2-normalize the
    result, and store that single averaged template plus how many samples contributed to it.
    Extend `RosterEntry` with a `sampleCount: number` field (alongside the `model`/`modelVersion`
    fields already planned in A4). This is the minimal change that keeps the existing single-
    vector cosine matcher unmodified — storing multiple embeddings per person and aggregating
    scores at match time is the more thorough alternative but changes both the roster shape and
    `matchEmbedding()` more substantially, and isn't necessary for a hackathon-scale roster.
  - A successful match against one of the allowlisted entries produces `identityStatus: resolved`
    and may reach automatic `clear`. **Anyone not matching an allowlisted entry produces
    `identityStatus: no_match`, which reaches `challenge` — never automatic `clear`.** This is not
    the same as automatic denial: a `no_match` result still goes through the existing human
    approve/deny flow on the phone, consistent with the rest of this design. If the intent is
    truly "automatically deny everyone else with no human step," that is a different behavior
    from what's built anywhere in this system today (suppression, alerting, and this decision
    matrix are all human-in-the-loop by design) and would need its own explicit decision — flagging
    this distinction rather than assuming which one is wanted.
  - **Accuracy caveat, stated plainly rather than implied**: face matching cannot mathematically
    guarantee zero false accepts, especially against a provisional, uncalibrated threshold. The
    achievable target for this phase is: only the allowlisted identities are enrolled; only a
    model-compatible match above the provisional threshold reaches `clear`; all impostor test
    captures taken during A6's rehearsal stayed below threshold; ambiguous/low-quality captures
    fail closed into retake or manual review, never into `clear`. That is a tested, provisional
    guarantee, not an absolute one, and should be described as such to anyone relying on this demo.

**A6 — Provisional threshold**
- Capture several photos per enrolled person (varying lighting/angle/distance) and several
  deliberate impostor attempts against the roster — including, specifically, cross-checks between
  every pair of allowlisted people (each enrolled person must not match any *other* enrolled
  person) and attempts from people who are not on the allowlist at all.
- Pick `ACCESS_MATCH_THRESHOLD` from what's actually observed, and document it as **provisional**
  — not a calibrated operating point — in whatever run notes accompany this session's work.
  `roster.ts`'s existing comment already says this default is a placeholder; the fix is picking
  a better provisional number with actual impostor data behind it, not certifying it as final.

**A7 — End-to-end acceptance, tested separately**
1. Known face → automatic `clear`, no human prompt (`identityStatus: resolved`, known).
2. Unknown face → `challenge` → Telegram alert → approve/deny on phone → correct audit entry
   (`identityStatus: no_match`).
3. Single-face rejected capture (no face / too small / poor quality) → `capture_rejected`, phone
   shows a retake prompt, no verdict computed from a garbage embedding, no premature escalation.
4. Multiple detected faces vs. one door-open entry → tailgating verdict still fires, using
   `detectedFaceCount`, even when identity resolution itself is rejected/incomplete (the A2 fix,
   tested explicitly since this is the regression risk the original draft would have introduced).
5. Backend unavailable (kill/misconfigure the script deliberately) → `backend_unavailable`, a
   visibly distinct "verification unavailable, manual review required" state, escalation never
   suppressed.
6. Backend returns malformed output (wrong dimension, non-finite values, an unrecognized `device`
   value, missing `detectedFaceCount` — deliberately injected in a test) → `backend_error`, same
   distinct treatment as #5, confirming the strict output-validation in A1 actually catches it
   rather than silently passing it through or misreporting `device`.
7. Roster containing an entry from an unrecognized/incompatible model → rejected/skipped at match
   time, not silently compared (the A4 fix).
8. Enrollment without the shared secret → rejected, roster unchanged, regardless of bind host;
   enrollment with a client-supplied vector via the badge-mode shape → rejected as a face
   enrollment, accepted only via the badge-specific path if that mode is kept.
9. Per-person acceptance, once the allowlist names are confirmed (§A5 demo allowlist): for each
   allowlisted person, enroll from multiple accepted captures, then confirm a fresh verification
   capture of that person matches only their own roster entry and reaches automatic `clear` — not
   another allowlisted person's entry.
10. Impostor rejection: capture several people who are *not* on the allowlist; every valid
    capture produces `identityStatus: no_match` and reaches `challenge`, never `clear` or
    `expected`.
11. Roster contents check: confirm the roster contains exactly the intended allowlisted
    identities, each with compatible model metadata (A4) and no persisted source images.
12. `npm run build && npm test` — confirm no regression in the existing access-control suite
    (**note**: `mcp-tools/node_modules` must be restored via `npm ci` first — Node/`@types/node`,
    `zod`, and `vitest` are all currently unresolved in this checkout, so build/test results are
    not yet authoritative), plus new tests covering 1–11 above.

### Phase B — `face-npu` on the laptop, as an opt-in feasibility spike (only after A works)

Not "swap CPU for QNN and continue" — treated as a spike with a documented outcome, because QNN
provider availability, operator support, and model conversion are not guaranteed to work on this
setup without checking first.

- Verify the QNN execution provider is actually installed and loadable, independent of Hermes.
- Run the exact model selected in Phase A on the QNN provider standalone; compare its output
  vectors against the CPU run within a defined numerical tolerance, and measure latency.
- Decide a concurrency-safety strategy before enabling this against a live GenieX instance —
  a plain check of `gateway_state.json`'s `active_agents` field is a check-then-act race, not a
  guarantee; prefer an actual inter-process lock/lease if one can be added cheaply, and if not,
  keep this backend opt-in/demo-only with an explicit accepted-risk note rather than a silent
  race.
- Never silently fall back from a failed NPU call to CPU or to `unknown` — a failed NPU attempt
  is a `backend_error`, using the same distinction built in Phase A3.
- Test one controlled concurrent GenieX run (trigger a capture while an agent turn is in flight)
  and confirm the chosen mitigation behaves as intended.
- **Document one of three outcomes**: viable and safe as built; viable only when serialized
  against GenieX; not viable on this hardware/SDK combination. Any of the three is an acceptable
  result of this phase — this is a spike, not a commitment.

### Phase C — phone-NPU native app (stretch goal, only if time remains after B)

- Resolve the Hexagon-version documentation conflict first (five-minute check against a primary
  source).
- Confirm the chosen face model's actual memory/runtime footprint on Android NNAPI/QNN before
  committing to this phase — do not assume the earlier LLM-memory blocker doesn't apply without
  checking.
- Build a minimal native Android app (or WebNN-in-browser pipeline if mature enough) that runs
  the same face-detection + embedding step on-device and sends only the embedding (never the
  photo) to `/api/access/capture` **for verification only**, tagged with `model`/`modelVersion`/
  `device`. This is a new, authenticated submission path, not the same trust level as today's
  image-based capture — require the shared secret and reject embeddings whose `model`/
  `modelVersion` don't match `ACCESS_MODEL_ID`/`ACCESS_MODEL_VERSION` (A1). Add a session/
  correlation id so a native capture and the existing browser-based approval UI can agree on
  which challenge they're both looking at.
- **Enrollment from the phone app, if built, goes through `/api/access/enroll`, never through
  `/api/access/capture`** — keep the two endpoints' semantics separate rather than adding an
  operation field to a single endpoint and inferring intent. Enrollment already requires a name
  and the mandatory shared secret from A3; a bare embedding POST to `/capture` must never be
  interpretable as "enroll this person."
- The existing `phone.html` approval UI can stay as the human-decision surface even if capture
  moves to a native app — approval and capture are separable concerns, and only capture needs to
  move.
- **Verification**: the same end-to-end scenarios as Phase A, plus confirming the laptop's NPU
  graph stays flat during a phone capture. Report that finding accurately — it shows no
  laptop-NPU activity, it does not by itself prove the phone dispatched inference to its NPU. If
  time permits, add Android-side execution-provider telemetry for a stronger claim.

